          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, including hybrid retrieval combining lexical and vector search.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\n  - pattern/Hybrid-Search\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Two independent sources covering retrieval strategies; not yet further corroborated.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g., BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets, often via reciprocal rank fusion, to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\nThis approach consistently outperforms either method alone on queries mixing rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs) with natural language intent. It has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems.\\n\\n## Reciprocal Rank Fusion\\n\\nReciprocal rank fusion is a robust merging strategy used to combine result sets from multiple retrieval methods.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval pattern overview and production adoption in RAG systems, eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-07\\n\u0022}"
